# Local scratch.  Move unwanted-but-not-deletable files here: reference/ is
# ignored, so nothing in it reaches the repository, and nothing is destroyed.
reference/
v2/

# Downloaded datasets (run experiments/download_cifar.py to fetch)
cifar/

# Experiment outputs.
#
# `outputs/*` rather than `outputs/`: ignoring the DIRECTORY would make git skip
# it entirely, and no `!` negation inside it could ever take effect.  Ignoring the
# CONTENTS lets the evidence below be tracked while caches and legacy reports stay
# out.
#
# Tracked on purpose (FINDINGS.md cites these as its evidence; without them a
# fresh clone has a conclusions page pointing at files that do not exist):
#   outputs/abl_*.txt    ablation reports  (sections 5, 6, 8, 11)
#   outputs/abl_*.png    their figures
#   outputs/sub_01_*     the substrate verification suite (sections 2, 3, 8)
# Everything else under outputs/ is ignored: outputs/abl_cache/ (the model caches)
# and every report or figure no document names.  experiments/cifar_07_4d_rgb.py
# READS outputs/cifar_01_baseline_report.txt, so that pair must not be moved or
# deleted without updating the reader.
# The patterns below are ANCHORED with a leading slash on purpose.  Unanchored,
# `outputs/*` and its negations match a directory named `outputs` at ANY depth,
# so `experiments/outputs/abl_something.txt` would have been whitelisted too.
# Verified with `git check-ignore -v`: an unanchored negation did let a nested
# copy through.  Anchoring keeps the whitelist to the repository's own outputs/.
/outputs/*
!/outputs/abl_*.txt
!/outputs/sub_01_*.txt
# Added when the missing artifacts were found: five scripts PRINTED their results and saved
# nothing, so their numbers existed only in a terminal, and the CIFAR line's flagship number
# lived in an untracked report that a comparison script then HARD-CODED.  A number that
# cannot be cited cannot be checked, which is what made the CIFAR comparison unresolvable
# (extension FINDINGS.md 12.32).  These are tracked for the reason stated above -- a document
# names them -- and `cifar_02_regularized_report.txt` is the one 12.32 quotes.
# The `nd_*` reports were dropped from the whitelist entirely.  Only three exist --
# nd_04_himal_pipeline, nd_05_himal_comparison, nd_07_physical_verify -- and no document
# names any of them.  (An earlier edit here listed six `nd_NN_report.txt` files that do
# not exist: written from memory, and caught by listing the directory.)
!/outputs/cifar_01_baseline_report.txt
!/outputs/cifar_01_baseline_report_PRE_SEED.txt
!/outputs/cifar_02_regularized_report.txt
!/outputs/cifar_02_regularized_report_UNSEEDED.txt
!/outputs/cifar_02_regularized_report_PRE_SEED.txt
!/outputs/cifar_03_3layer_reg_report.txt
!/outputs/cifar_03_3layer_reg_report_PRE_SEED.txt
!/outputs/cifar_04_large_data_report.txt
!/outputs/cifar_04_large_data_report_PRE_SEED.txt
!/outputs/cifar_05_mlp_baseline_report.txt
!/outputs/cifar_05_mlp_baseline_report_PRE_SEED.txt
!/outputs/cifar_06_three_way_compare_report.txt
!/outputs/cifar_06_three_way_compare_report_PRE_SEED.txt

# The FIVE figures a document actually cites, whitelisted by name.  Plots are not
# evidence the way a report is: a plot is regenerable from the report's numbers by
# re-running its script, and no claim depends on the image itself.  Everything else
# under outputs/ that is a figure stays untracked, which took about sixty files out
# of the repository -- most of them the extension's, none of which any document cites.
# The name-by-name form is deliberate: a pattern like `!/outputs/*_plot.png` would
# re-admit every figure the moment a script emitted one.
!/outputs/abl_01_scaling_diffusion_shareaxis_plot.png
!/outputs/abl_01_capacity_split_diffusion_shareaxis_plot.png
!/outputs/abl_02_coupling.png
!/outputs/abl_02_parallel.png
!/outputs/abl_06_task_sweep_plot.png

# The EXTENSION's evidence set, and it is EIGHT REPORTS, named one by one.
#
# The rule here used to be `!/outputs/ext_*.txt`, which tracked every report all fifty-one
# scripts ever wrote.  The extension's artifact map cites the SCRIPTS, not the reports, so
# forty-five of those fifty-three files were named by no document -- a run's intermediate
# output rather than its evidence.  A reader who wants one can produce it by running the
# script the document does cite; shipping it saved them a run and cost the repository a
# directory of files nothing pointed at.
#
# These eight are the ones a document quotes BY NAME as its evidence
# (BASE_CORRECTIONS.md and FINDINGS.md sections 9 and 12.15-12.19).
!/outputs/ext_25_budget_matched.txt
!/outputs/ext_26_base_family.txt
!/outputs/ext_27_nonmonotone.txt
!/outputs/ext_29_reservoir.txt
!/outputs/ext_30_equal_params.txt
!/outputs/ext_32_residual_mode.txt
!/outputs/ext_33_gate_power.txt
!/outputs/ext_34_deployment_harm.txt

# Four STALE ablation-A figures, ignored BY NAME rather than by widening the rule
# above.  No script in the repository emits a `_smoke` or a non-`_shareaxis` plot
# name any more, and a repository-wide search finds these four cited in no
# document -- so they are orphans of an earlier version of
# experiments/abl_01_capacity_split.py.  The figures FINDINGS.md actually cites
# (abl_01_scaling_diffusion_shareaxis_plot.png and
# abl_01_capacity_split_diffusion_shareaxis_plot.png) are tracked and must stay
# so: a clone that cannot see them has a conclusions page pointing at files that
# do not exist, which is the defect the whitelist above exists to prevent.
# These patterns come AFTER the `!` negations on purpose -- a later pattern wins.
/outputs/abl_01_capacity_split_plot.png
/outputs/abl_01_scaling_plot.png
/outputs/abl_01_capacity_split_diffusion_smoke_plot.png
/outputs/abl_01_scaling_diffusion_smoke_plot.png

# The extension keeps its OWN model cache.  It must never write into
# outputs/abl_cache/: that cache is keyed by (task, arch, arm, seed, epochs,
# budget) and ablation C LOADS from it to reuse ablation A's models, so a
# colliding key would make abl_03 evaluate an extension model as if it were a
# base model (DESIGN section 9.1).  Covered by /outputs/* already; written out
# so nobody "tidies" it away.
outputs/ext_cache/

# experiments/cifar_07_4d_rgb.py used to resolve a nested experiments/outputs/ relative to
# itself, so a run from inside experiments/ wrote there instead of to the repository's own
# outputs/.  That script now follows the convention and the nested directory is gone; this
# rule stays only so that a stale copy left on someone's disk is not picked up.
experiments/outputs/

test_outputs/

